    "question": {
      ":question/id": "893a3dea-aca6-502a-8459-3475a2d5f36c",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{8}{x^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 4=4(1+(\\frac{1}{4})x^{2})\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(2)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{x^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= 2\\int \\frac{1}{1+(\\frac{x}{2})^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 2(2)\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
